
       GIT BRANCHING

What is a Branch?

A branch is an isolated line of development in Git.

It allows us to work on new features or make changes without affecting
the main branch.

By default, Git creates a main branch (previously often called master).

Example:

main
 |
 |------ feature1
 |
 |------ feature2


MAIN / MASTER BRANCH

The main branch contains the main source code of the project.

Changes made directly in the main branch affect the main project.


FEATURE BRANCH

A feature branch is created to work on a specific feature separately.

Example:

main
  |
  |------ feature-login

You can work on the login feature without affecting the main branch.

Changes made in a feature branch will not affect the main branch
until the branch is merged.


IMPORTANT:

✓ Every branch is isolated.

✓ Changes in one branch do not automatically affect another branch.

✓ A branch is useful for working on new features safely.

✓ After completing the work, branches can be merged.


========================
       GIT COMMANDS
========================

1. View available branches:

git branch


2. Create a new branch:

git branch branch-name

Example:

git branch feature-login


3. Switch to another branch:

git switch branch-name

Example:

git switch feature-login


Older method:

git checkout branch-name

Example:

git checkout feature-login


4. Create and switch to a branch:

git checkout -b branch-name

Example:

git checkout -b feature-login


========================
        EXAMPLE
========================

First, check branches:

git branch

Create a new branch:

git branch feature1

Switch to that branch:

git switch feature1

Now make changes and commit them.

These changes will remain in feature1 and will not affect
the main branch until they are merged.


MAIN IDEA:

Branch = Separate workspace for development.

Git branching helps developers work on different features safely
without disturbing the main project.